{"sourceSha256": "0452eaed29404355b55f8bf201d5f9ab9204d5fb4db67d82345103ac59d44ae8", "text": "ROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE\n\ni ar aie\n\nSETTING Suburban Clinic\nPATIENT You are an elderly patient who was recently hospitalised following an acute episode\nof pain (in the lower left abdomen) and rectal bleeding due to diverticulitis (you have\na long history of constipation). On discharge, you were told to consult your doctor\nbut were given no further information regarding the diagnosis or future prospects.\nYou are extremely worried that this condition will recur, and you have heard of\npeople dying from perforations in such cases.\nTASK ¢ Seek reassurance from the doctor about what the hospital found.\n¢ Find out if you are likely to have another episode.\nGet advice on controlling the condition and preventing recurrence.\ne Ask the doctor to explain why the hospital gave you antibiotics.\n© Be difficult to reassure, as it was a very frightening experience, but be prepared\nto listen to the doctor's advice.\n|\n’ i}\n| © Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessmont FEBRUARY 2015 _\n\nSETTING\n\nDOCTOR\n\nTASK\n\nNDIDATE CARD NO. 4\n\nCA MEDICINE\n\nOET FEBRUARY 2015 9 a\n\nSuburban Clinic\n\nAn elderly patient has come to you after an acute episode of diverticulitis. He/she |\nhad acute onset of pain in the left iliac fossa and rectal bleeding for which he/she\n\nwas hospitalised. The hospital report sent to you indicated no perforation,\n\nabscesses, peritonitis or fistula. The patient is extremely anxious as he/she was not\ngiven any information about the condition on discharge.\n\ne Reassure the patient that the hospital report indicates no perforation, etc.\n\n* Explain to the patient that the condition could recur.\n\n* Stress the importance of dietary modification to avoid constipation. However,\nexcessive use of aperients (purgatives) should be avoided.\n\n¢ Explain why the antibiotic treatment was prescribed (bacterial infection of the\ncolon can cause diverticular disease).\n\n¢ Advise the patient to return if rectal bleeding or bad abdominal pain recurs; a\n\nsurveillance colonoscopy might be the next step if bowel habit alters or bleeding\nretums.\n\n© Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment FEBRUARY 2015", "pages": [{"number": 1, "text": "ROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE\n\ni ar aie\n\nSETTING Suburban Clinic\nPATIENT You are an elderly patient who was recently hospitalised following an acute episode\nof pain (in the lower left abdomen) and rectal bleeding due to diverticulitis (you have\na long history of constipation). On discharge, you were told to consult your doctor\nbut were given no further information regarding the diagnosis or future prospects.\nYou are extremely worried that this condition will recur, and you have heard of\npeople dying from perforations in such cases.\nTASK ¢ Seek reassurance from the doctor about what the hospital found.\n¢ Find out if you are likely to have another episode.\nGet advice on controlling the condition and preventing recurrence.\ne Ask the doctor to explain why the hospital gave you antibiotics.\n© Be difficult to reassure, as it was a very frightening experience, but be prepared\nto listen to the doctor's advice.\n|\n’ i}\n| © Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessmont FEBRUARY 2015 _\n\nSETTING\n\nDOCTOR\n\nTASK\n\nNDIDATE CARD NO. 4\n\nCA MEDICINE\n\nOET FEBRUARY 2015 9 a\n\nSuburban Clinic\n\nAn elderly patient has come to you after an acute episode of diverticulitis. He/she |\nhad acute onset of pain in the left iliac fossa and rectal bleeding for which he/she\n\nwas hospitalised. The hospital report sent to you indicated no perforation,\n\nabscesses, peritonitis or fistula. The patient is extremely anxious as he/she was not\ngiven any information about the condition on discharge.\n\ne Reassure the patient that the hospital report indicates no perforation, etc.\n\n* Explain to the patient that the condition could recur.\n\n* Stress the importance of dietary modification to avoid constipation. However,\nexcessive use of aperients (purgatives) should be avoided.\n\n¢ Explain why the antibiotic treatment was prescribed (bacterial infection of the\ncolon can cause diverticular disease).\n\n¢ Advise the patient to return if rectal bleeding or bad abdominal pain recurs; a\n\nsurveillance colonoscopy might be the next step if bowel habit alters or bleeding\nretums.\n\n© Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment FEBRUARY 2015", "extraction": "ocr", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 2163.0, "height": 2800.0, "blocks": [{"text": "ROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE", "lines": [{"text": "ROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE", "x": 0.04669, "y": 0.05071, "width": 0.70643, "height": 0.01286, "confidence": 0.95, "words": [{"text": "ROLEPLAYER", "x": 0.04669, "width": 0.15442, "confidence": 0.914}, {"text": "CARD", "x": 0.20897, "width": 0.06472, "confidence": 0.957}, {"text": "NO.", "x": 0.28433, "width": 0.03837, "confidence": 0.951}, {"text": "4", "x": 0.33148, "width": 0.01063, "confidence": 0.963}, {"text": "MEDICINE", "x": 0.64309, "width": 0.11003, "confidence": 0.964}], "uncertainWords": []}]}, {"text": "i ar aie", "lines": [{"text": "i ar aie", "x": 0.88997, "y": 0.02071, "width": 0.07582, "height": 0.01821, "confidence": 0.32, "words": [{"text": "i", "x": 0.88997, "width": 0.00092, "confidence": 0.457}, {"text": "ar", "x": 0.91817, "width": 0.02219, "confidence": 0.402}, {"text": "aie", "x": 0.95146, "width": 0.01433, "confidence": 0.103}], "uncertainWords": ["i", "ar", "aie"]}]}, {"text": "SETTING Suburban Clinic\nPATIENT You are an elderly patient who was recently hospitalised following an acute episode\nof pain (in the lower left abdomen) and rectal bleeding due to diverticulitis (you have\na long history of constipation). On discharge, you were told to consult your doctor\nbut were given no further information regarding the diagnosis or future prospects.\nYou are extremely worried that this condition will recur, and you have heard of\npeople dying from perf